  "mechaber/part-001.txt": {
    "1:main": "The laws of nolad on Shabbat. And in it 6 seifim: An egg that was born on Shabbat is forbidden even to move it, and even if it became mixed in a thousand — all are forbidden; and one may press a vessel upon it so that it not break, provided he does not touch it:",
    "2:main": "A Shabbat that is after a Yom Tov, or before it — an egg that was born on one is forbidden on the other:",
    "3:main": "Fruits that fell from the tree on Shabbat are forbidden on that day, and in the evening they are permitted immediately:",
    "4:main": "Animal food — there is no concern of fixing a utensil; and therefore it is permitted to cut off even with a knife tough straw or hay, and to pick one's teeth with it; but a splinter that is not animal food — even to take it in order to pick one's teeth with it is forbidden:",
    "5:main": "It is permitted to move fragrant woods in order to smell them, and to wave them for a sick person, and to rub them in order to smell — both tough and soft:",
    "6:main": "One who apportions portions to the members of his household on Shabbat may cast lots to say: whoever's lot comes out shall have such-and-such a portion; and this is when the portions are equal, and he does so only in order to appease them so as not to cast jealousy among them; but with others it is forbidden, since they are strict with one another they will come to measuring and weighing; but to give a large portion against a small one and cast lots upon them — even with the members of his household, and on a weekday — forbidden on account of gambling (kubya); and some say that with his sons and the members of his household it is permitted to cast lots even for a large portion against a small one, since they are not strict: {Rama: And it is forbidden to cast lots on Shabbat even through a non-Jew [Maharil]:}"
  },
  "baer-heitev/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "To move it. A decree on account of Shabbat after Yom Tov, when it is then forbidden d'oraisa on account of preparation:",
    "2:_": "That he not touch. See siman 310 seif 6 and what is written there:",
    "3:_": "Forbidden. See siman 501 and what is written there in s.k. 9:",
    "4:_": "Forbidden. And if he cut it off — liable, Rambam chapter 11; and see Magen Avraham:",
    "5:_": "And he rubs it. It must read: and he cuts it off and rubs it to smell, etc. In Shakhne'ahag he inclined to be stringent: specifically cutting off a little so it smell — permitted; but if he breaks off a piece from it in order to give to another — forbidden, see there. And Yam Shel Shlomo wrote chapter 4 that this cutting off is permitted only by hand but not with a knife; and it was not permitted with a knife except for animal food, see there. And Taz in siman 501 seif 4 disagrees with Yam Shel Shlomo in this and permits even with a knife, see there; and Magen Avraham in siman 501 raised that specifically to add scent — permitted, but forbidden to create a new scent, see there:",
    "6:_": "Lots. But for Kaddish or who goes up to the Torah — permitted, for many poskim hold that on Yom Kippur they cast lots for who slaughters, who throws. Magen Avraham, see there; and see Taz and Beit Aharon:"
  },
  "turei-zahav/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "An egg that was born, etc. — will be explained, with God's help, in the laws of Yom Tov simanim 513 and 514, see there; and what is written \"and he shall not touch them\" — see siman 310 seif 6 for the reason on this:",
    "3:_": "Fruits that fell, etc. — forbidden. A decree lest he climb and detach; and in the evening permitted, and one need not wait until they are prepared, since no melacha was done in them they did not decree in this:",
    "5:_": "And he cuts it off and rubs it to smell, etc. — as it must read; and Rashal wrote in chapter \"one who brings\" siman 9 that this cutting off is permitted only by hand but not with a knife; and they did not permit with a knife except for animal food; and see siman 501 that we do not rule thus:",
    "6:_": "And some say with his sons, etc. — this is the view of Rambam, as is explained in Beit Yosef in the distinction between Ri'f and Rosh and Tur on one side and Rambam on the other: Rambam follows the plain meaning of the sugya in chapter \"one who asks\"; but Ri'f and Rosh rejected the halacha since it is established according to Rav, who said permitted to lend interest to his sons, etc.; and we do not rule like Rav in chapter \"if not,\" for we say lest he come to be drawn after; and if so, here too forbidden with a large portion against a small one on account of gambling; here too they decreed lest he come to be drawn after; and the Rav HaMagid labored to reconcile Rambam's view and said that interest is different since it is d'oraisa, unlike the prohibition of gambling which is from their words — they did not decree regarding sons and household members; and one may wonder: if so, what did the gemara challenge at first — \"if so, with his sons and household members too\"? For the prohibition with others is on account of that which Shmuel said: members of a fellowship who are strict with one another transgress on account of measuring and weighing, etc. — meaning lest they come to this; and all those prohibitions are only rabbinic when you examine them; and we may also say they did not decree regarding sons and household members on account of this prohibition which is rabbinic; and further we find Rambam himself decreed in a decree of lest he come to be drawn after even in rabbinic matters, as in siman 499 where Beit Yosef wrote that Rambam wrote in a responsum not to bless on the cup on the eighth day and give to a child lest he come to be drawn after, as is explained there — this reason; and they learned there from what is stated in the gemara that one does not bless time on the cup in the Temple and give to a minor lest he come to be drawn after; and regarding the twenty cubits it is a d'oraisa prohibition to drink, and on the eighth day it is only rabbinic — nevertheless he equated them; and we may say the same here. And it appears to me the distinction between d'oraisa and rabbinic in this is not that Rambam follows his view that he holds in chapter \"any interest\" that a person may lend interest from his sons in order to accustom them to the taste of interest and hide their money to lend to a non-Jew with interest and it will be sustenance for them; and in truth there is no prohibition of interest in this since he is not strict with them and gives them the interest as a gift; and so Rambam explicitly regarding interest in this, since he is not strict with them; and on this we conclude there \"and it is not so, lest they come to be drawn after to lend even to an Israelite with interest,\" for the reason will be pleasing in their eyes; and if so we are not concerned in this except regarding the prohibition of interest lest he accustom them intentionally to this, for habit will harm him afterward; unlike other prohibitions — if he is not strict with household members and gives them as a gift even a large portion against a small one we are not concerned lest he come to be drawn after, for there is no reason for this decree, for he does not intend to accustom them to this; and this itself that the gemara answers is the reason as R' Yossi said: Rav permitted to lend, etc. — meaning his reason is since a person is not strict — therefore there is no interest here; likewise there is no prohibition of gambling here; and even though we reject Rav regarding interest on account of lest he come to be drawn after — here this does not apply; and we rule well according to this view that a person is not strict — it is not rejected; and where the decree of lest he come to be drawn after does not apply we certainly rule like Rav that there is no prohibition of gambling here; and therefore Rambam concluded the reason well \"since they are not strict\" — in this is the main learning from Rav according to the halacha; as it appears to me, correct in my humble opinion:"
  },
